# Who has to look at a change before it lands.
#
# This file on its own does nothing. GitHub reads it to *request* reviews
# automatically, and that is all it does until somebody turns on "Require
# review from Code Owners" in the branch protection rule for `main`. Both
# halves are needed and only one of them lives in the repository; the other is
# item 3 of the checklist in `docs/supply-chain.md`. A CODEOWNERS file with no
# rule behind it is a list of people who get notified, which is worth having
# and is not a control.
#
# WHY THESE PATHS AND NOT EVERY PATH
#
# The temptation is to write `* @67plays` and be done. That is a worse control
# than this one, for a reason that has nothing to do with trust: a rule that
# fires on every pull request stops being read. When the CODEOWNERS review is
# the same ceremony for a typo in a comment as for a change to signature
# verification, the reviewer learns that the ceremony carries no information,
# and the one that mattered gets the same glance as the four hundred that did
# not. The paths below are the ones where a mistake is silent, permanent, or
# both -- where "it passed CI and looked fine" is not evidence, because the
# failure mode of the code is that it keeps working while being wrong.
#
# Everything else in this repository is reviewed the ordinary way. That is not
# a statement that the rest does not matter; it is a statement that attention
# is finite and this is where it buys the most.
#
# A note on ownership by one person: @67plays owns the repository and writes
# most of it, and GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull request.
# So for @67plays's own changes this file does not produce a second pair of
# eyes -- it produces a blocked merge until somebody else looks, or an
# administrator override. That is the honest state of a project with one
# maintainer, and the useful half is the other direction: a change to any path
# below by @20plays, @resslr, or anyone added later cannot merge without the
# owner having seen it. As the project grows, the fix is to add a second owner
# to these lines, not to delete them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release infrastructure
#
# The paths from which a compromised change reaches every deployment at once.
# Everything else on this list can hurt the servers running this code; this
# section can hurt the servers that have not installed it yet, because it is
# what decides what "installing it" means. A workflow file is executable code
# that runs on a machine holding registry credentials, and a change to one is a
# change to what that machine does with them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/                       @67plays
/.github/workflows/             @67plays
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @67plays
/Dockerfile                     @67plays
/.dockerignore                  @67plays
/deploy/                        @67plays
/scripts/                       @67plays

# The advisory allowlist deserves its own line even though `/.github/` already
# covers it, because a diff to this file is always a decision to tolerate a
# known vulnerability and should never be read as configuration noise. See
# `scripts/audit-gate.mjs`.
/.github/dependency-audit-allowlist.json  @67plays

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cryptography and identity
#
# Code whose bugs do not announce themselves. A verification function that
# returns `true` too often passes every test written against valid input, ships,
# and is discovered by whoever was looking for it rather than by anybody here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/server/src/crypto.rs           @67plays
/server/src/auth.rs             @67plays
/server/src/routes/opaque.rs    @67plays
/server/src/routes/identity/    @67plays
/server/src/routes/identity_continuity.rs @67plays
/server/src/revocation.rs       @67plays
/telium-protocol/               @67plays
/server/src/updates.rs          @67plays

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Federation
#
# The trust boundary with other people's servers. Every byte that arrives here
# was written by somebody who does not answer to this project, and the code
# that decides whether to believe it is the code that decides whether a remote
# server can act as one of this server's users.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/server/src/federation/         @67plays
/server/src/gateway/            @67plays
/server/src/perms.rs            @67plays
/server/src/memberships.rs      @67plays
/server/src/ratelimit.rs        @67plays
/server/src/security_headers.rs @67plays
/sfu/                           @67plays

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Migration and relocation
#
# Data moving between servers, and schema moving under live data. The thing
# that makes these different from ordinary code is that they are not
# idempotent and they are not reversible: a migration that drops the wrong
# column has already dropped it by the time anybody reads the log line, and no
# amount of rolling the binary back brings it back.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/server/migrations/             @67plays
/server/src/migration.rs        @67plays
/server/src/migration/          @67plays
/server/src/relocation.rs       @67plays
/server/src/db.rs               @67plays

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup and continuity
#
# The code that is exercised for real exactly once, on the worst day anybody
# involved will have. It is reviewed here because it has no other feedback
# loop: a backup path that silently writes nothing looks identical from the
# outside to one that works, right up until somebody needs it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/server/src/backup/             @67plays
/server/src/objectstore.rs      @67plays
/server/src/cold.rs             @67plays

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security-facing documents
#
# Not code, and on this list anyway. SECURITY.md is the promise made to
# somebody who found a vulnerability and is deciding whether to tell us
# privately or publish; a quiet edit to the reporting instructions is a quiet
# edit to that promise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/SECURITY.md                    @67plays
/docs/supply-chain.md           @67plays
